Yes—the contrast itself is perfectly campfire-natural. Going sincere → teasing → sincere can make a toast feel alive rather than polished into blandness. The issue isn’t tonal whiplash so much as **whether the teasing clearly signals shared affection**.

If Marcus, Priya, and Dave routinely joke about the brewery incident themselves, the middle is much more likely to land as inside-baseball *in the room*. Their reactions—laughing, groaning, raising a glass—will instantly tell outsiders, “This is established lore.” A written draft looks harsher because it lacks those social cues.

That said, an outsider could still briefly hear pettiness because the roast stacks several sharp signals:

- all three are named;
- the unpleasant work is vividly quantified;
- their illnesses are called “mysterious”;
- “We see you” implies judgment;
- “freeloaders” repeats the accusation after the joke has landed.

So: **not inherently petty, and probably not awkward if the targets genuinely own the joke—but it has more bite than ordinary affectionate ribbing.** Delivery matters enormously. A grin, brisk pacing, and visible warmth toward those three will frame it as group folklore; any pause suggesting “I’ve waited two years to say this” changes the temperature fast.

The overall structure works as a toast. The real test is whether Marcus, Priya, and Dave will feel included in the joke rather than used as comic villains. Based on your added context, I’d call it **inside-baseball with a slightly sharp edge**, not straightforward score-settling.
